Arquebus

"But wait!" I hear you cry. "Slash 'em already has firearms!"

But that's exactly the problem: Slash'em has MODERN firearms such as
assault rifles. They are an anachronism.

By contrast, the arquebus appeared in western Europe at roughly the
same time plate armor did;  it is NOT an anachronism. In fact, it is my
understanding that later versions of plate were specifically developed
to counter the threat of primitive firearms, said development
continuing until increase in muzzle velocity made the necessary weight
of metal too heavy for a human to wear in battle;  at which point
people forsook armor until the advent of Kevlar.

New Items:
Arquebus (one-shot weapon)
Arquebus Ammunition Sack (item, 1-N charges)

New Monsters:
Arquebusiers (soldier sub-class)
Orc Arquebusiers (Uruk-hai sub-class)

The arquebus itself is a weapon that can be used exactly once.  Because
it is notoriously inaccurate, it suffers a severe to-hit penalty.  When
it DOES hit, it causes tremendous damage, enough to kill a Captain in
one shot.

More important on the battlefield than it's killing ability was the
terror and confusion inspired by this smoke-bellowing monstrosity;  to
model this,  the weapon has a secondary effect of Fear on any creature
within a certain distance of the firing weapon. The duration of this
fear depends on the creature's intelligence level:  Horses and
comparable animals should be panicked for ten-plus rounds,
man-intelligence should be panicked for a few, while an arch-lich
should have no fear at all.

The weapon is unreliable. accordingly, there is a 1-in-2.5 chance of a
misfire and a 1-in-20 chance of the weapon backfiring and exploding. An
exploding arquebus deals severe damage to the user, blinds the user,
and moreover destroys the weapon.

A blessed arquebus will always fire correctly (0% chance of misfire or
backfire) and has a better chance to hit the target; however, no
arqubus will ever have a to-hit higher than 0, unless enchanted.

A cursed arqubus is guaranteed to always misfire, with a 1-in-4 chance
of exploding.

*

Arquebus ammunition.

A sack of arquebus ammunition allows one to reload an arquebus so that
it may be re-used.  This is done by a)pplying the sack to the weapon.
As with opening cans, this will take MANY turns, modeling the slow
re-loading rate of the original weapon (approximately 20 minutes
between shots).

A cursed sack will not successfully load the weapon, and may break it.


A blessed sack will reload in one turn.

*

Arquebusiers

This is a very rare soldier type, rarer than captains, but are never
generated in groups less than 5.  Preferably, they will be stacked
together in a region and fire their weapons in a volley en masse.
Since the weapons are not magical, neither reflection nor magic
resistance have any impact on the fired shots, any more than they do on
arrows or drats.  Even a veteran adventurer is going to be in trouble
if they are simultaneously hit by 5 or 10 rounds from an arqubusier
squad.

Once fired, the arqubusiers will turn to flee, looking to break contact
until they can reload their weapons.  Thus, it is wise that they be
generated with regular soldiers who can cover their escape.  The
arquebusiers will have standard soldier armor and a close-in melee
weapon, such as a short sword, but will not carry polearms or shields.


A fixed encounter with large groups of these soldiers at Croesus'
castle or The Castle itself is advisable.  Mariliths may also be
generated with one of these weapons, although after use they will
discard them.

*
Orc Arquebusiers

A variant on the soldier arquebusier, but generated more randomly.
Their primary purpose is to keep the adventurer guessing.  They have an
increased chance of cursed weaponry. 

